homelab-roundcube (0.1.10) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Release v0.1.10

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 17:21:55 -0500

homelab-roundcube (0.1.10) unstable; urgency=medium

  * recipient_blocking: EXPERIMENTAL -- give "Blocked Addresses" its own
    taskbar icon (a peer of Mail/Contacts/Settings) instead of only
    being reachable as a buried Settings tab, per direction. Registers
    a real new Roundcube task via rcube_plugin_api::register_task() (a
    genuine, generically-wired plugin API -- the same mechanism real
    Kolab plugins like calendar/tasklist use, confirmed against the
    actual installed core, not a hack) and reuses the exact same
    list/search/unblock page and PHP methods the Settings tab already
    uses -- no duplicated logic, just a second way to reach it. The
    Settings tab itself is left in place, unchanged, for now
  * recipient_blocking: real gotcha worth flagging for future
    maintenance -- rcube_plugin::register_task()'s wrapper sets
    $this->mytask, which rcube_plugin::register_action() then
    unconditionally uses to prefix every SUBSEQUENT register_action()
    call from the same plugin instance. Calling the wrapper as the
    "obvious" way to add the new task would have silently broken the
    existing mail/settings actions (wrong prefix). Worked around by
    calling the lower-level rcube_plugin_api methods directly wherever
    task-scoping actually matters, leaving $this->mytask untouched

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 17:21:39 -0500

homelab-roundcube (0.1.9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * recipient_blocking: fix a real bug found via live testing -- a
    message with multiple comma-separated addresses on its To: header
    (e.g. several addresses under the same domain catch-all grant) only
    ever had the FIRST one blocked; extract_address() explicitly took
    only explode(',', $raw)[0]. Now extracts and blocks every address
    found (Delivered-To if present, all instances, otherwise every
    address in To:), one API call per address -- confirmed the backend
    itself needed no change, its per-address ownership check already
    passes for each address independently, sequential calls already
    worked, the plugin just never made more than one
  * recipient_blocking: add a real toolbar icon (Font Awesome's "ban"
    glyph, already present in Elastic's self-hosted icon font, unused
    elsewhere in this skin) via a small plugin-owned CSS file loaded
    with include_stylesheet() -- no build step, matches the exact
    mechanism core's own Reply/Forward/Delete buttons use so it
    inherits theme color automatically. Also the mobile-layout fix: the
    icon is what was missing at narrow widths, not a separate
    breakpoint issue
  * recipient_blocking: the tooltip and post-block/unblock toast now
    name the actual address(es) involved instead of a generic sentence
    -- server-side via Roundcube's real gettext `vars` substitution
    mechanism, client-side (which has no such support) via manual
    string replacement using the same $address-style placeholder token
    for consistency
  * recipient_blocking: the already-blocked check for a message's
    address list is now one bulk fetch of the caller's full blocked
    list per message render instead of one lookup per candidate address

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 16:36:34 -0500

homelab-roundcube (0.1.8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Release v0.1.8

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 15:52:00 -0500

homelab-roundcube (0.1.8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * recipient_blocking: fix the toolbar Block button being permanently
    disabled for every message, found only by a real end-to-end browser
    test (confirmed via nginx access logs -- every plugin.block_recipient
    POST on record was from scripted testing, none from a real browser
    click, in Brave and Chrome alike). Root cause: Elastic's message
    preview renders in an <iframe>, but the toolbar the button lives in
    is part of the outer window's own document; message_headers_output
    (which computed the address/already-blocked state) only ever fires
    while rendering the framed preview, so that state stayed trapped in
    the iframe's own separate rcmail.env and never reached the outer
    window where rcmail.register_command(...) needed to run. Now: the
    session-wide "is SSO available" check moved to init() (no longer
    waits on any preview), and the framed preview pushes its per-message
    state up to the outer window via parent.rcmail (the same pattern
    roundcube-core itself already uses for this exact iframe/parent
    split)
  * recipient_blocking: clear the block target immediately on any
    message-list selection change (drop to zero, jump to multiple, or a
    new single selection whose preview hasn't loaded yet) instead of
    leaving the button pointing at a message that's been moved, deleted,
    or is no longer in view -- moving/archiving the previewed message is
    routine, not an edge case, and this is an action whose entire point
    is blocking a sender's future mail, so stale state here is a real
    correctness issue, not a cosmetic one. Also guards against an
    out-of-order async preview load clobbering newer state by checking
    the previewed message's uid before applying a sync

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 15:51:34 -0500

homelab-roundcube (0.1.7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Release v0.1.7

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 14:06:20 -0500

homelab-roundcube (0.1.7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * recipient_blocking: fix a real, reproducible failure of every AJAX
    action (block/unblock/list) found only by an actual end-to-end
    browser-session test, not by inspection -- Debian's roundcube-core
    ships no vendor/autoload.php; GuzzleHttp\Client is only ever loaded
    within a request by rcmail_oauth.php's own conditional include, and
    only when that file's code actually runs (e.g. IMAP/SMTP connection
    setup), never guaranteed for a plugin's own standalone action. Now
    loads Guzzle itself the same way, and logs (rather than silently
    swallowing) any \Throwable from the API call
  * recipient_blocking: add one bounded retry for pure transport-level
    API failures -- php-fpm here has no `curl` extension loaded, so
    Guzzle falls back to its plain PHP-stream handler (no connection
    pooling/retry of its own); confirmed by repeated live testing this
    occasionally hits a bare ECONNREFUSED against homelab-api under this
    environment's real concurrent load even though the very next attempt
    succeeds. A real 4xx/5xx response is never retried, only a true
    transport failure (no response at all)

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 13:42:19 -0500

homelab-roundcube (0.1.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Add recipient_blocking plugin: block/unblock one of your own
    addresses from the message toolbar and Settings; calls homelab-api's
    existing self-service recipient-access API using the same JWT
    already used for IMAP/SMTP, no new credential
  * postinst: symlink the new plugin into /var/lib/roundcube/plugins/
    (Debian's roundcube-core resolves plugins there, not directly under
    /usr/share/roundcube/plugins/ where the package installs them) --
    without this every page request 500s with "Failed to load plugin
    file"; postrm removes the symlink on package removal

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 13:35:43 -0500

